	\section{Introduction}
\label{intro}

Document vectorization plays a vital role in many natural language processing (NLP) tasks. One of the most popular document vectors is the \emph{term frequency-inverse document frequency} (TF-IDF) feature vector \cite{robertson1976relevance}. It can offer a robust baseline in many NLP tasks, including genre classification, albeit task specific design may improve the performance. \cite{karlgren1994recognizing,kessler1997automatic,wolters1999exploring,stamatatos2000text,dewdney2001form,lee2002text,freund2006towards,petrenz2009assessing,webber2009genre}.

%Term-frequency-based document vectorization makes  :
%(a) occurrences of each term are mutually independent, and (b)
%a document is treated as a ``bag of words'' and different permutations of the same set of words are considered to be same.

these models can also take abstract long-term information into consideration. However, these are semi-supervised or supervised methods, which prevent them from training document embeddings from unlabeled data. %Thus it is appropriate to test our document vectors against the similar unsupervised document vectors PV-DM and n-gram TF-IDF, both of which have shown good performance in many tasks. 
The effectiveness of the proposed document vectors was evaluated on genre classification of documents in three corpora, and their performance was compared with that of TF-IDF vector and PV-DM. Our results show that in most cases, our document vector significantly outperforms n-gram TF-IDF vector and PV-DM.
